Costco's gross margin percentage rose to 11.12% in 2025 from 10.92% in 2024. [1] That is an increase of 20 basis points; excluding the effect of gasoline price deflation on net sales, the increase was 11 basis points, to 11.03%. [2]

The main positive driver was core merchandise categories, which added 19 basis points, primarily due to fresh foods and the co-branded credit card program. Offsetting this, a LIFO charge for higher merchandise costs reduced the percentage by seven basis points, and warehouse ancillary and other businesses reduced it by one basis point. [2] Foreign currency changes also reduced gross margin by about $224, tied to Other International and Canadian operations. [2]

Measured against core merchandise sales alone, gross margin rose 16 basis points, mainly from fresh foods and foods and sundries, partly offset by non-foods. [2] By segment, the U.S. performed similarly to the consolidated results, while Canada and Other International gross margin increased mainly on core merchandise and warehouse ancillary and other businesses. [3]

The filing also notes that gasoline carries a lower gross margin percentage, so falling gasoline prices have the inverse effect of rising ones, which lower the percentage. [4] That helps explain why the reported increase is larger than the ex-gasoline one.